The Google Nest Learning Thermostat is a landmark example of eco-feedback and persuasive technology applied to sustainable behaviour change. The device uses machine learning to understand a household's thermal patterns and occupancy habits, automatically optimising heating schedules to reduce energy waste. The "green leaf" icon appears when users select energy-saving temperatures, providing immediate positive reinforcement that nudges behaviour without restricting choice. Evaluations by the UK Behavioural Insights Team found that Nest users reduced their heating demand by 6-16%, with the highest savings among those who most actively engaged with the eco-feedback features. The thermostat also provides monthly energy reports comparing consumption to similar homes, leveraging social comparison — a powerful persuasive design strategy. From a product design perspective, the Nest demonstrates how embedding behavioural science principles (feedback, rewards, social norms, default settings) into a physical product can meaningfully shift user behaviour toward sustainability. The device's sleek design, intuitive interface, and passive learning capabilities show that sustainable behaviour interventions need not compromise user experience.